# outline_user

Manages an Outline workspace account by UUID. Outline assigns the computed `id` during creation; do not set it in configuration. This resource manages the account's display name, role, and suspension state, not group memberships or authentication.

Destroy suspends the account by default, retains its memberships and content, and removes it from Terraform state. Creating another resource with the same email will fail. Import the retained account's UUID instead.

Use an unrestricted API key owned by an active admin. The provider checks the caller before modifying an account and refuses to modify the API-key owner's account. Use a different admin's key to manage that user. The resource targets Outline 1.10.1 only.

For provisioning accounts that sign in through OIDC, see the [OIDC guide](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/glitchedmob/outline/latest/docs/guides/oidc).

## Example usage

{{ tffile "examples/resources/outline_user/resource.tf" }}

`suspended` is required and has no default. Set it to `false` to activate the account or `true` to suspend it. This is the desired final state, including after any role change. Activation does not sign the user in or verify their IdP account.

`email` is required and immutable. Invitations use a lowercase, normalized email; state preserves equivalent configured casing. Any configuration change to `email` requires replacement, including a casing-only edit. Outline email renaming requires a separate confirmation flow outside this provider. Do not use replacement as an email rename.

`name` is optional and computed, with no default. Creation uses `Pending user` as the invitation name when you omit it. This avoids treating an email containing `www.` as a URL, which Outline rejects in display names. Configured names must contain 1 to 255 Unicode code points and must not contain a URL. After creation, omission leaves the display name unmanaged and reports its current value. OIDC sign-in can change the name. If you configure `name`, Terraform reapplies that value after such a change.

`role` is optional and computed, with a default of `member`. Valid values are `admin`, `member`, `viewer`, and `guest`. Removing it from configuration restores `member` on the next apply. All four API roles are tested against Outline 1.10.1, but server edition, licensing, API-key scopes, and policies can reject a role change. The provider reports those errors.

`suppress_email` is optional and computed, with a default of `true`. It only controls the invitation sent during creation. Changing it on an existing resource does not send or resend an email.

`delete_permanently` is optional and computed, with a default of `false`. It selects the destroy behavior described below.

## Creation and updates

Creation checks every page of the admin-visible user list, including pending and suspended accounts. It also checks the invitation response for `unsent` invitations to catch an account created between the lookup and invitation. Create never adopts or modifies an existing account. Import its UUID instead.

Outline 1.10.1 converts a guest invitation to a member invitation. The provider reads the stored account after inviting it and reconciles the requested role, name if configured, and explicit final suspension state.

Changing a suspended account's role temporarily activates it, changes the role, then restores the configured `suspended` value. This can briefly restore access. If a follow-up write fails and the desired final state is suspended, the provider attempts to suspend the account again. That recovery is best-effort. Inspect the account if the apply reports an error.

Once Outline returns a newly created account's matching UUID, the provider retains it in state before follow-up validation, reads, or writes. Terraform still taints failed creates. Fix the API error, then run `terraform untaint outline_user.alice` using your resource address before applying again. The next apply reconciles the existing account. Do not retry replacement blindly. Default destruction retains the email, and Create then refuses it. If state was lost or the invitation response did not supply a usable UUID, check the workspace and import any created account before retrying.

Refresh removes an account from state only after verifying absence. In Outline 1.10.1, a missing `users.info` target returns `403 authorization_error`. The provider independently verifies an active admin and checks the complete `filter = all` user list, including suspended accounts, before treating that response as absence. A bare 403, a listed UUID, failed verification, or malformed pagination remains an error and retains state. An unexpected `users.info` 404 also requires the complete list check, since a missing route or proxy response does not prove the account was deleted.

## Destroy behavior

With `delete_permanently = false`, destroy suspends the account, retains the user, memberships, and content in Outline, and removes the resource from Terraform state. To manage it again, import the UUID and set `suspended` explicitly in configuration. Recreating the same email does not reactivate or adopt the retained account.

With `delete_permanently = true`, destroy calls `users.delete`. This is irreversible through the provider. Outline 1.10.1 anonymizes and soft-deletes the user; this is not a guarantee of a full database purge or erasure of retained content. It does not delete the user's documents. Apply the opt-in while the resource is still in configuration, then review the destroy plan. Removing the resource block first uses the last value stored in state.

Email replacement also uses the old resource's destroy policy. The default leaves the old account suspended and creates a different account for the new email. Memberships and content do not transfer. `create_before_destroy` cannot work around an existing-email conflict.

